WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
From TF-IDF to BM25.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:11.117
TF-IDF scores a document for a query by summing, over query terms, term frequency times inverse document frequency.

3
00:00:11.397 --> 00:00:15.920
A common idf is log(N / n), where N is the number of documents.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:29.085
It captures two good intuitions: a term that appears often in a document is probably what the document is about, and a term that appears in few documents is a better discriminator than one that appears everywhere.

5
00:00:29.365 --> 00:00:31.093
It also has two weaknesses.

6
00:00:31.373 --> 00:00:42.723
Term frequency counts linearly, so a document that says “license” twenty times scores twenty times higher on that term than one that says it once, though it isn’t twenty times more relevant.

7
00:00:43.003 --> 00:00:46.907
And long documents accumulate higher counts just by being long.

8
00:00:47.187 --> 00:00:49.171
BM25 fixes both.

9
00:00:49.451 --> 00:01:02.187
Robertson and Zaragoza’s survey “The Probabilistic Relevance Framework: BM25 and Beyond” (2009) derives it from a probabilistic model of relevance; here is the result.

10
00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:19.405
For a query Q and document D: score(D, Q) = sum over query terms q of idf(q) times tf(q, D) times (k1 + 1), divided by tf(q, D) + k1 times (1 minus b plus b times dl / avgdl).

11
00:01:19.685 --> 00:01:26.768
The idf is the Robertson and Sparck Jones weight with no relevance information: log((N minus n plus 0.

12
00:01:27.048 --> 00:01:29.011
5) / (n plus 0.

13
00:01:29.291 --> 00:01:30.101
5)).

14
00:01:30.381 --> 00:01:37.891
That goes negative for terms in more than half the documents, so Lucene uses log(1 + (N minus n plus 0.

15
00:01:38.171 --> 00:01:40.133
5) / (n plus 0.

16
00:01:40.413 --> 00:01:42.504
5)), which is always positive.

17
00:01:42.784 --> 00:01:45.067
You’ll use the Lucene form in the exercise.

18
00:01:45.347 --> 00:01:58.147
The (k1 + 1) in the numerator is a constant per term, so it doesn’t change the ranking; the survey notes that it is included so a single occurrence weighs the same as in the plain RSJ weight.
